From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Sotraidis <56282582+chrissotraidis@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 01:55:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] port: a pad map a host can read, and a render scale that can
 follow again

The vendored host interface paints its own controller screen and its own
resolution row, and both were log-only here. Two seams:

PortPadMapping/PortPadName/PortPadDetail expose the live Aurora pad map in
the same terms report_gamepad() prints: the device name, every pad button and
axis with what Aurora bound it to, and the dead zones, trigger mode and rumble
intensity. The table is rebuilt on every call, because a controller connect
reloads the whole mapping. An empty port is an empty map, not an error.

PortSetRenderScale(scale <= 0) used to hand 0 to aurora_set_frame_buffer_scale
and leave the scale pinned at whatever zero meant. It now means "follow the
window again": the pin is cleared, the derived scale and the row memo are
zeroed so the next derivation logs even at the same row count, and the call
returns. A positive scale still pins.

Aspect: PortSetTargetAspect takes a ratio, and PortFollowWindowShape keeps
calling PortSetWindowAspect every frame, which only writes while following, so
a pinned aspect genuinely survives and a non-positive target restores it.

The pad map's three definitions first sat inside the unnamed namespace that
opens above them, and a name in an unnamed namespace has internal linkage
however it is declared: the C names never reached the linker, and because
nothing in the file called them the compiler left them out of the object file
altogether. They now sit at namespace scope, between two blocks of the same
unnamed namespace, so the header's extern "C" declarations bind and
input.cpp.o exports _PortPadMapping, _PortPadName and _PortPadDetail.

Simulator Release links.
